{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "05-semilattices.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Semilattices: the laws of a merge",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Derive the three laws a merge must obey from the ways messages travel, and recover an order from any operation that obeys them.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Two copies (**replicas**) of some data, say the highest score seen in a game, live on two machines. Now and then each sends its state to the other, which **merges** it into its own: $x \\cdot y$. The network is unreliable in three ways:\n\n1. messages arrive in any order;\n2. a replica may merge a batch of messages before passing on the result;\n3. a message may arrive twice.\n\nWe want two replicas that have received the same messages to hold the same value, however the network behaved.\n\n> [!try]\n> Before reading on, turn each of the three hazards into an equation the merge must satisfy.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Three hazards, three laws",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Any order: $x \\cdot y = y \\cdot x$, **commutative**. Any grouping: $(x \\cdot y) \\cdot z = x \\cdot (y \\cdot z)$, **associative**. Twice: $x \\cdot x = x$, **idempotent**: merging what you already have changes nothing.\n\nEach law is needed. Here are natural merges, written as tables (row $x$, column $y$ holds $x \\cdot y$), each breaking one law. \"Keep the newer one\", $x \\cdot y = y$, depends on which message arrived last:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let K = op({a, b, c}; [a, b, c; a, b, c; a, b, c])",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "commutative(K)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "\"Average, rounded down\" depends on how the messages were batched:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let AVG = op({0, 1, 2}; [0, 0, 1; 0, 1, 1; 1, 1, 2])",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "associative(AVG)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "\"Add\" (capped at 2, to stay in the set) counts a duplicated message twice:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let ADD = op({0, 1, 2}; [0, 1, 2; 1, 2, 2; 2, 2, 2])",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fold(ADD; 1)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fold(ADD; 1, 1)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "\"Keep the larger\", $\\max$, survives all three:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let M = op({0, 1, 2}; [0, 1, 2; 1, 1, 2; 2, 2, 2])",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "semilattice(M)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Semilattice\n> A **semilattice** is a set with an operation that is **associative**, **commutative** and **idempotent**: an operation whose result does not depend on the order, the grouping or the repetition of what it combines.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Union on the subsets of $\\{a, b\\}$ is another; set elements are written as sets:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let U = op({{}, {a}, {b}, {a,b}}; [{}, {a}, {b}, {a,b}; {a}, {a}, {a,b}, {a,b}; {b}, {a,b}, {b}, {a,b}; {a,b}, {a,b}, {a,b}, {a,b}])",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "semilattice(U)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "identity(M)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "An **identity**, an element that changes nothing, is the natural starting state of a replica that has heard nothing yet: $0$ for $\\max$, $\\varnothing$ for union.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The order hiding in a merge",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$\\max$ merges $0, 1, 2$, which are ordered $0 < 1 < 2$; union merges sets, which are ordered by $\\subseteq$. In both, the merge is the join of the order.\n\n> [!try]\n> Given only the table of a semilattice, how would you get the order back? Ask when merging $x$ into $y$ tells $y$ nothing new.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] A semilattice is a partial order\n> Define $x \\le y$ when $x \\cdot y = y$. Idempotence makes it reflexive, commutativity antisymmetric, associativity transitive, and then $x \\cdot y$ is the least upper bound of $x$ and $y$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "order(M)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "order(U)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The union table gives back the powerset's diamond. The engine's `order` is proved to produce a partial order whose join is the operation (`semilattice_order`). Without the laws there is no order to find: averaging is refused, with the triple that breaks associativity.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "order(AVG)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "So the laws a merge needs and the laws of a join are the same three. A safe merge *is* the join of an order, \"knows at least as much as\", and every merge moves a replica up it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The same definitions as a class, and the order it induces, proved a partial order:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "class Semilattice (α : Type) where\n  merge : α → α → α\n  merge_comm  : ∀ (a b : α), merge a b = merge b a\n  merge_assoc : ∀ (a b c : α), merge (merge a b) c = merge a (merge b c)\n  merge_idem  : ∀ (a : α), merge a a = a\n\nexport Semilattice (merge merge_comm merge_assoc merge_idem)\n\n/-- The order a semilattice induces: a is below b when merging a into b changes nothing. -/\ndef Semilattice.le {α : Type} [Semilattice α] (a b : α) : Prop := merge a b = b\n\ntheorem Semilattice.le_refl' {α : Type} [Semilattice α] (a : α) : Semilattice.le a a := merge_idem a\n\ntheorem Semilattice.le_antisymm' {α : Type} [Semilattice α] (a b : α)\n    (h1 : Semilattice.le a b) (h2 : Semilattice.le b a) : a = b := by\n  unfold Semilattice.le at h1 h2\n  rw [← h2, merge_comm, h1]\n\ntheorem Semilattice.le_trans' {α : Type} [Semilattice α] (a b c : α)\n    (h1 : Semilattice.le a b) (h2 : Semilattice.le b c) : Semilattice.le a c := by\n  unfold Semilattice.le at *\n  rw [← h2, ← merge_assoc, h1]\n\n/-- So every semilattice is a partial order. -/\n@[reducible] def Semilattice.toPartialOrder (α : Type) [Semilattice α] : PartialOrder α where\n  le := Semilattice.le\n  le_refl := Semilattice.le_refl'\n  le_antisymm := Semilattice.le_antisymm'\n  le_trans := Semilattice.le_trans'",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The merge of two elements is above both, and below anything above both: it is their join.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem Semilattice.le_merge_left {α : Type} [Semilattice α] (a b : α) : Semilattice.le a (merge a b) := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove that $a \\le a \\cdot b$.",
      "leanSolution": "  unfold Semilattice.le\n  rw [← merge_assoc, merge_idem]",
      "hints": [
        "`unfold Semilattice.le` turns the goal into `merge a (merge a b) = merge a b`.",
        "Regroup with `merge_assoc` (right to left), then `merge_idem`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem Semilattice.le_merge_right {α : Type} [Semilattice α] (a b : α) : Semilattice.le b (merge a b) := by\n  unfold Semilattice.le\n  rw [merge_comm a b, ← merge_assoc, merge_idem]\n\ntheorem Semilattice.merge_le {α : Type} [Semilattice α] (a b c : α)\n    (h1 : Semilattice.le a c) (h2 : Semilattice.le b c) : Semilattice.le (merge a b) c := by\n  unfold Semilattice.le at *\n  rw [merge_assoc, h2, h1]",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Answer `true` or `false`, or with an element.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let W = op({0, 1, 2, 3}; [0, 1, 2, 3; 1, 1, 3, 3; 2, 3, 2, 3; 3, 3, 3, 3])",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "semilattice(W)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Is $W$ a semilattice?",
      "hints": [
        "Check the diagonal first, then whether the table is symmetric."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "identity(W)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "What is the identity of $W$?",
      "hints": [
        "The row that copies the header."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "join(order(W), 1, 2)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "In the order $W$ gives, what is the join of $1$ and $2$? Read it off the table before computing it.",
      "hints": [
        "The join of the order is the merge: row 1, column 2."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "associative(K)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Is \"keep the newer one\" associative?",
      "hints": [
        "$(x \\cdot y) \\cdot z = z$ and $x \\cdot (y \\cdot z) = z$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> A merge that ignores order, grouping and repetition is associative, commutative and idempotent: a semilattice. Every semilattice is an order, $x \\le y$ exactly when $x \\cdot y = y$, and its merge is the join. A table decides the three laws, and a failure is a triple or a pair you can point at.\n\n$\\max$ and union are merges of this kind. Next: the merges real systems use, for records of several fields and for \"the last write wins\", and whether they are joins too.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
